//! Server-Sent Events (SSE) wire encoder for the Datastar package.
//!
//! Datastar drives hypermedia updates over a long-lived SSE stream: the server
//! pushes named events, and each event carries one or more `data:` lines that
//! the browser runtime interprets (patching elements, patching signals, and so
//! on). This file is the low layer that turns those pieces into the exact bytes
//! an SSE consumer expects. It knows nothing about Datastar's semantics; the
//! higher level modules assemble the event type and the `data:` payload lines,
//! then hand them here to be framed.
//!
//! The SSE framing rules this encoder implements come straight from the
//! `text/event-stream` format: each field is written as `field: value\n`, the
//! recognised fields are `event`, `id`, `retry`, and `data`, and a single blank
//! line (an extra `\n`) terminates the event so the client dispatches it. An
//! event may carry several `data:` lines, which the client joins with newlines
//! into one payload. See [`buildFrame`] for the assembly and ordering.
//!
//! Two deliberate design decisions are worth calling out. First, optional
//! fields are omitted rather than sent empty: an absent `id` and a `retry` that
//! equals the protocol default ([`ds_const.DEFAULT_RETRY_MS`]) are simply not
//! written, keeping each frame as small as possible on a stream that stays open
//! for the life of the page. Second, the integer to text conversion is done by
//! a small hand-rolled routine ([`intToStr`]) rather than pulling in a heavier
//! formatting path, since the only number this file ever emits is the retry
//! interval.

import string;
import list;
import bytes;
import collections.string_builder;
import ds_const;

/// Formats a non-negative `int` as its base-10 decimal `string`.
///
/// A minimal, allocation-lean integer to text routine used only for the SSE
/// `retry:` value, so it intentionally handles just the cases that field needs.
/// It special-cases zero, then counts the decimal digits, allocates a buffer of
/// exactly that many bytes, and fills it back to front by repeatedly taking the
/// last digit (`n % 10`) and adding 48, the ASCII code for `'0'`. The buffer is
/// reinterpreted as a `string` with no copy. It assumes `val` is not negative;
/// a negative input would not produce a leading minus sign, but the only caller
/// ([`buildFrame`]) passes a millisecond retry value which is never negative.
pub fn intToStr(val: int): string {
    if (val == 0) { return "0"; }
    let n = val;
    let digits = 0;
    while (n > 0) { digits = digits + 1; n = n / 10; }
    let p = bytes.alloc(digits);
    let i = digits - 1;
    n = val;
    while (i >= 0) {
        bytes.write_byte(p, i, (n % 10) + 48);
        n = n / 10;
        i = i - 1;
    }
    return p as string;
}

/// Joins a Datastar data-line key with its value into one payload line.
///
/// A Datastar `data:` line is composed of a key prefix (for example the
/// `selector `, `mode `, or `elements ` constants in [`ds_const`], each of which
/// already ends in a trailing space) followed by the value. This helper is just
/// that concatenation, kept as a named function so callers assembling the list
/// of data lines read clearly and so the shape stays in one place. Note it does
/// NOT prepend the SSE `data: ` field marker or append the newline; that framing
/// is added later by [`buildFrame`] when each element of the list is written.
pub fn dataLine(key: string, val: string): string {
    return key + val;
}

/// Encodes one complete SSE event into a single ready-to-write `string`.
///
/// The frame is assembled in the order an SSE parser expects: the `event:` line
/// first, then an optional `id:` line, then an optional `retry:` line, then one
/// `data:` line per entry in `dataLines`, and finally a blank line that
/// terminates the event so the browser dispatches it.
///
/// Optional fields are elided to keep the stream lean: the `id:` line is written
/// only when `eventId` is non-empty, and the `retry:` line only when `retryMs`
/// differs from [`ds_const.DEFAULT_RETRY_MS`], since re-sending the default on
/// every frame would waste bytes on a connection that stays open. Each entry of
/// `dataLines` is expected to be a fully composed payload (typically built with
/// [`dataLine`]); this function wraps it with the `data: ` marker and a newline.
/// A null entry, which `List.get` surfaces as an optional, is treated as an
/// empty payload via `?? ""` so a gap in the list never aborts the frame.
///
/// Building goes through a [`string_builder.StringBuilder`] to avoid repeated
/// intermediate string allocations. The builder is explicitly freed with
/// `sb.delete()` before returning, after its contents have been materialised
/// into the owned `out` string, so no builder memory outlives the call.
pub fn buildFrame(eventType: string, dataLines: List<string>, eventId: string, retryMs: int): string {
    let sb = string_builder.StringBuilder();

    sb.append("event: ");
    sb.append(eventType);
    sb.append("\n");

    if (eventId != "") {
        sb.append("id: ");
        sb.append(eventId);
        sb.append("\n");
    }

    if (retryMs != ds_const.DEFAULT_RETRY_MS) {
        sb.append("retry: ");
        sb.append(intToStr(retryMs));
        sb.append("\n");
    }

    let i = 0;
    while (i < dataLines.size()) {
        sb.append("data: ");
        sb.append(dataLines.get(i) ?? "");
        sb.append("\n");
        i = i + 1;
    }

    sb.append("\n");

    let out = sb.toString();
    sb.delete();
    return out;
}
